{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1361446-obfuscate-vs-nightmare-let-s-make-clarity","id":1361446,"title":"Obfuscate vs Nightmare - Let's make clarity","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-01-17T05:58:06-08:00","id":1361446,"title":"Obfuscate vs Nightmare - Let's make clarity","content":"Hi,<br /> <br /> I'd like to collect in this post your insight about what can and what cannot be obtained with the two disciplines under a mechanical perspective:<br /> <br /> Nightmare 4 allows you to create Hallucinations. This is something also Obfuscate 5 allows. So what's the real difference between them? I'll male some practical example:<br /> <br /> 1. Can I create the hallucination (with Nightmare 4) of me or someone else not being there anymore the same way I can create the hallucination of a moster being there? If not, what is the justification?<br /> 2. Can I create the hallucination of a wall of shades, infinite dark tunel, a wall which can conceal my presence?<br /> 3. As Nightmare 4 is affecting all the 5 senses, can I create the hallucination of an abyss opening under the victim feet where he think to be falling down? If the hallucintion is affecting all those present in the area (using it through Dread Presence) how the witnesser outside the hole would see the falling PNC? Like falling down and then disappeaing devoured by the darkness, or forever falling down withou never disappearing?<br /> 4. Someone hallucinated to being burst into flames, will be feeling pain, heat and everything, when he will disproof the hallucination?<br /> 5. With Obfuscate 5 I can conceal, doors, windows, stairs, making handrail appears as snakes. Is it all that also achievable with Nightmare 5 (although with more time)?<br /> 6. One of my real nightmare when I was child was to walk through an endless corridor...how will it be represented with Nightmare 4 or Obfuscate 5? When the NPC is walking through it it will have the distorted perception of walking faster than he should, isn't it?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":7079,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","user":"Marcus","date":". 01-17-2020, 09:02 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","name":"Marcus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-17T07:36:25-08:00","id":1361461,"title":"","content":"1. No not really. As for why not? The hallucination is supposed to be something the victim fears and it's only supposed to affect ONE object or addition within in area the victim can see. Not only is &quot;disappearing&quot; completely the opposite of adding something or someone, you should use Obfuscate if you want to make yourself or an object disappear.<br /> 2. With Nightmare 4, I would need more context of the situation before making a decision. As a &quot;security&quot; feature for someone to arrive later and encounter, I'd say absolutely not. As a method of torturing someone you captured and is in the room already, probably.<br /> 3. Yes you could create such a hallucination but you don't get to control what others see or how they'll react or not. (The hallucination takes on a life of it's own.) They may &quot;see&quot; the person disappear within the context of the hallucination as that's what their mind fills in. Maybe they'll see a black dragon fly out from the chasm beneath the falling person and eat him and proceed to breathe a gout of flame on them. Maybe they'll see a swarm of corpses crawl out of the pit. Maybe they maintain enough of their lucidity and see that the person is falling but never disappears from view to make them question their own sanity.<br /> 4. <span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">The hallucination lasts for a scene and doesn't cause any physical damage. </span></span></span>He will start to disbelieve it when he realizes he isn't actually burning, that's the purview of the storyteller as to when that happens. Coming to that realization may be what ends the scene, or maybe it morphs into him seeing other things start to burn as well.<br /> 5. Not really. See #1 for why things don't just disappear. And I'd also say no for time constraints, Waking Nightmare lasts one scene, Oubliette lasts for a minimum of one week, you don't have a lot of time to be playing with every last minutiae in an attempt to deliberately circumvent the restrictions hard coded into the power. You want to have complete control and highly detailed illusions, use Oubliette.<br /> 6. They would probably just see the hall keep going. Here's the thing with Oubliette, if they continue walking, they'll eventually run into something that causes them to see the environment for how it really is. They'll either literally run straight into the building's door or wall or trip over a piece of furniture. Maybe they'll kick the wall in frustration and see the illusion break for a moment. And at that point they may realize it's an illusion and react accordingly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5466-tsusasi","name":"tsusasi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5466_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1028"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-17T09:06:39-08:00","id":1361488,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>tsusasi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1361446-obfuscate-vs-nightmare-let-s-make-clarity?p=1361461#post1361461\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1. No not really. As for why not? The hallucination is supposed to be something the victim fears and it's only supposed to affect ONE object or addition within in area the victim can see. Not only is &quot;disappearing&quot; completely the opposite of adding something or someone, you should use Obfuscate if you want to make yourself or an object disappear.<br /> 2. With Nightmare 4, I would need more context of the situation before making a decision. As a &quot;security&quot; feature for someone to arrive later and encounter, I'd say absolutely not. As a method of torturing someone you captured and is in the room already, probably.<br /> 3. Yes you could create such a hallucination but you don't get to control what others see or how they'll react or not. (The hallucination takes on a life of it's own.) They may &quot;see&quot; the person disappear within the context of the hallucination as that's what their mind fills in. Maybe they'll see a black dragon fly out from the chasm beneath the falling person and eat him and proceed to breathe a gout of flame on them. Maybe they'll see a swarm of corpses crawl out of the pit. Maybe they maintain enough of their lucidity and see that the person is falling but never disappears from view to make them question their own sanity.<br /> 4. <span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">The hallucination lasts for a scene and doesn't cause any physical damage. </span></span></span>He will start to disbelieve it when he realizes he isn't actually burning, that's the purview of the storyteller as to when that happens. Coming to that realization may be what ends the scene, or maybe it morphs into him seeing other things start to burn as well.<br /> 5. Not really. See #1 for why things don't just disappear. And I'd also say no for time constraints, Waking Nightmare lasts one scene, Oubliette lasts for a minimum of one week, you don't have a lot of time to be playing with every last minutiae in an attempt to deliberately circumvent the restrictions hard coded into the power. You want to have complete control and highly detailed illusions, use Oubliette.<br /> 6. They would probably just see the hall keep going. Here's the thing with Oubliette, if they continue walking, they'll eventually run into something that causes them to see the environment for how it really is. They'll either literally run straight into the building's door or wall or trip over a piece of furniture. Maybe they'll kick the wall in frustration and see the illusion break for a moment. And at that point they may realize it's an illusion and react accordingly.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I can understand quite everything but the poijnt number 3. If I recall correctly you can dictate how the hallucination is acting while you are there, and it will just act by himself when you leave the area.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","name":"Marcus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-17T09:26:28-08:00","id":1361496,"title":"","content":"But you still don't dictate their responses. And if you're &quot;there&quot; and not reacting to the group hallucination &quot;appropriately&quot; (that you don't experience because you can't turn the power on yourself) then obviously something is off.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5466-tsusasi","name":"tsusasi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5466_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1028"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}